Acute Biliary Colic
Acute Urgent Care
Postprandial RUQ pain, Radiation to right shoulder, Mobile gallstones on US, Absence of fever

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-020
Reason for Visit: Severe, episodic right upper stomach pain radiating to the back following a heavy meal.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe right upper quadrant pain accompanied by nausea and restlessness, starting an hour after dinner. Urgent assessment was required to provide acute analgesia and to reliably differentiate self-limiting biliary colic from acute cholecystitis or ascending cholangitis, ensuring the patient was safe to remain at home overnight rather than requiring emergency surgery.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I had a big steak for dinner, and an hour later this awful, gripping pain started under my right ribs and shot into my shoulder.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the onset of severe, constant right upper quadrant pain that began roughly 45 minutes after consuming a high-fat meal. The pain is described as a deep, gripping ache that radiates directly to the right shoulder blade. The patient has been extremely restless, pacing the room attempting to find a comfortable position, but nothing relieves the pain. There is significant nausea and two episodes of non-bilious vomiting. The pain has been severe for the last 3 hours but is currently beginning to plateau. The patient explicitly denies feeling feverish, experiencing chills, or noticing any yellowing of the skin.
Functional Status: Pacing, distressed by pain, but fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk; supportive spouse present.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient is uncomfortable and restless, frequently shifting positions, with mild diaphoresis noted.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals localized right upper quadrant tenderness on deep palpation, but notably lacks rebound tenderness or involuntary guarding. A Murphy's sign is negative (no inspiratory arrest upon deep palpation of the right subcostal margin). There is no clinical jaundice or scleral icterus. Cardiovascular and respiratory exams are unremarkable. Temperature is normal, indicating an absence of systemic inflammatory signs. Expected investigations would reveal mobile echogenic gallstones with acoustic shadowing within the gallbladder, without gallbladder wall thickening on a transabdominal ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Home environment is safe and appropriate for conservative management.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took oral paracetamol 2 hours ago with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Biliary Colic - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Cholecystitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of fever, a negative Murphy's sign, and the lack of localized peritoneal guarding, indicating obstruction without active wall inflammation.

Peptic Ulcer Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain radiating strictly to the right shoulder blade and the clear postprandial onset following a fatty meal, typical of gallbladder contraction.

Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain localizing to the right upper quadrant/shoulder blade rather than retrosternal/jaw, normal oxygen saturations, and pain reproducible by abdominal palpation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s presentation is a textbook case of biliary colic: postprandial severe right upper quadrant pain radiating to the right shoulder, extreme restlessness, and nausea, all without the systemic signs of infection (fever) or localized peritonitis (negative Murphy's sign). The urgent after-hours assessment successfully confirmed the absence of acute cholecystitis or cholangitis. By providing robust parenteral analgesia and ruling out an immediate surgical emergency, the visit prevented an unnecessary late-night Emergency Department presentation while ensuring the patient's severe symptoms were safely and effectively managed in the home setting.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ketorolac 30mg Intramuscularly (IM) for severe biliary pain and Ondansetron 4mg ODT for nausea. Pain decreased from 8/10 to 3/10 within 30 minutes.
Positive Behavioral Change: By actively choosing a low-fat diet and avoiding heavy, greasy meals, you stop forcing your gallbladder to contract forcefully, which directly prevents these painful stones from being pushed into the duct and causing another attack.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 1-2 weeks for an outpatient upper abdominal ultrasound and possible general surgery referral for elective cholecystectomy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the pain persists continuously for more than 6 hours, or if you develop a high fever, uncontrollable shivering, or notice your eyes turning yellow, you must proceed immediately to the Emergency Department as this indicates a serious infection or permanent blockage.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is a symptomatic episode caused by gallstones temporarily blocking the exit of the gallbladder.
Clinical Course: The pain will typically resolve completely within 4 to 6 hours as the gallbladder relaxes and the stone falls back into the sac. However, attacks are likely to recur if dietary changes are not made.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Rest at home tonight. Stick to clear fluids until tomorrow morning. If you wake up with a high fever or the pain returns and does not settle, do not wait; go straight to the hospital.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have given you a strong anti-inflammatory injection because traditional painkillers often do not work well for gallbladder spasms. Your pain is already starting to fade because the muscle is relaxing.
Actionable Steps: Avoid all full-fat dairy, fried foods, and rich meats starting immediately. Book an appointment with your regular doctor next week to get an ultrasound scan to physically map the stones in your gallbladder.

